.TH TERMINAL-ROUTER 1 "September 2026" "terminal-router 1.2.0" "SysLinuxOS Manual"
.SH NAME
terminal\-router \- record tmux terminal sessions for AI\-assisted router work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B terminal\-router
.RI [ claude | codex | command ]
.br
.B terminal\-router workspace
.RI [ claude | codex ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B terminal\-router
opens a two\-pane tmux workspace by default. The left pane is a normal shell
for connecting to a router or switch. The right pane runs a Claude Code or
Codex observer that automatically analyses new recorded output and answers
questions typed by the operator. No separate assistant terminal or manual log
selection is required.
.PP
Each workspace uses a private tmux server and captures only the original left
pane under
.IR ~/.claude\-logs .
The observer follows a workspace\-specific link across pause/resume; opening
another workspace does not redirect it to another device or to its own output.
User tmux configuration and global recording hooks are not loaded into this server.
.PP
The intended workflow is an interactive SSH session on a Cisco or Huawei
router. The assistant has no access to the device and never needs any: the
operator types, the assistant reads the log and suggests, the operator decides
what to run.
.PP
All configuration is per\-user and confined to
.IR $HOME .
Nothing is enabled by installing the package; each user must opt in explicitly
and acknowledge the security notice once.
.PP
.B install
and
.B launch
warn, without blocking, if neither
.B claude
nor
.B codex
is found on PATH, since the log they set up then has no AI assistant to
read it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR workspace " [claude|codex]"
Open the integrated workspace. With no arguments, terminal\-router does the
same. Automatic engine selection prefers Claude Code, then Codex. Click a pane
to switch or use Ctrl\-b followed by an arrow key. Initial focus is on the router.
.TP
.BR claude ", " codex
Open the workspace using exactly the named engine. A missing engine produces
an error; it is not silently replaced by the other engine. The CLI must already
be installed and authenticated.
.TP
.B install
Set up the shell and tmux hooks for the calling user. Idempotent. Logs every
interactive terminal.
.TP
.B launch
Start one self\-contained, one\-off logged tmux session instead. No
.I ~/.bashrc ,
.I ~/.zshrc
or
.I ~/.tmux.conf
edit is made: the hooks are set on that single session only and disappear
with it, so every other terminal \(em Terminator included \(em is
unaffected. Detach with Ctrl\-b d to fall back to a plain shell in the same
window; logging continues while detached. Use pause to suspend capture.
Exiting ends the session.
.TP
.B remove
Undo them. The marked blocks are deleted from
.I ~/.bashrc
and
.IR ~/.tmux.conf ,
both Bash and Zsh startup files are checked, each backed up as
.IR file .terminal\-router.bak ,
the crontab rotation entry is dropped, and the log directory can optionally be
deleted.
.TP
.B status
Report which hooks are active, whether rotation is configured, whether
.B claude
or
.B codex
is on PATH to read the log, how many logs exist and which panes are current,
including integrated workspaces on private tmux servers.
.TP
.B tail
Follow the newest pane log, switching when the latest symlink changes.
.TP
.B pause
Suspend capture in the calling tmux pane. Also available in launch sessions.
.TP
.B resume
Resume capture in the calling pane into a new file. An already active pipe
is left unchanged.
.TP
.B doctor
Read-only checks for setup, permissions, latest log and live panes. Returns
1 for detected errors and 0 otherwise; warnings alone do not cause failure.
.TP
.B menu
Configuration menu and alternate launch modes. The desktop entry opens the
integrated workspace directly; use menu for global setup or removal.
.TP
.B help
Show usage.
.SH WORKSPACE CONTROLS
.TP
.B F9
Suspend recording of the original router pane. This works from either pane,
including while the router shell is inside SSH; no command is sent to the device.
.TP
.B F10
Resume recording into a new file, preserving the observer's log link.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b d
Detach from tmux. Recording and the observer continue while detached.
The launcher prints a workspace\-specific reattach command; its socket is at
.IR ~/.claude\-logs/workspace\-ID/tmux.sock .
.SH OBSERVER COMMANDS
Type the following in the right pane:
.TP
.I question
Ask about the latest captured router output. Up to five questions can wait
while an AI request runs. Questions are not sent to the router as commands.
.TP
.B Enter
Request an analysis immediately, including when automatic analysis is off.
.TP
.B :auto off
Stop automatic AI requests; manual questions remain available. An already
submitted request can still finish. This does not pause disk recording.
.TP
.B :auto on
Enable automatic analysis, or retry after fixing a CLI/authentication error.
.TP
.B :quit
Close the observer. The router pane remains open and keeps its recording state.
.PP
Automatic requests group changes after two quiet seconds, or five seconds of
continuous output, with a minimum ten\-second interval between requests. Model
response time is additional. Unchanged output makes no new request. Only one
request runs at a time, with a 120\-second timeout. Errors stop automatic mode.
.PP
Context is limited to the last 80 lines and 16 KiB of the router log, plus the
previous exchange. It is not a complete session history. Pausing capture with
F9 prevents new automatic analysis; a request already submitted may complete.
Manual questions during pause use the last captured output.
.SH SHELL FUNCTIONS
After install, shell shortcuts delegate to the pause and resume commands.
Inside a logged pane,
.B logpause
detaches logging from that pane and
.B logresume
resumes it into a new file. Use them around sensitive work.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.bashrc
Marked block that enters tmux on interactive terminals and defines
logpause/logresume. Uses
.I ~/.zshrc
when the login shell is zsh.
.TP
.I ~/.tmux.conf
Marked block with the pipe\-pane hooks.
.TP
.I ~/.claude-logs/
Log directory, mode 0700. Contains one 0600 file per pane and the symlink
.I latest
pointing at the newest.
.TP
.I ~/.claude-logs/workspace-ID/current
Stable link to this workspace's current router log. Workspace directories are
private (0700). The AI pane is never recorded by the workspace launcher.
.TP
.I ~/.config/terminal-router/consent
Records that this user accepted the security notice.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TR_CLAUDE_MODEL
Optional Claude model override for the observer.
.TP
.B TR_CODEX_MODEL
Optional Codex model override for the observer.
.PP
Without these variables the chosen CLI uses its default model. The observer
retains CLI authentication, but disables shell tools and configured integrations
and ignores project configuration. Codex also uses a read\-only sandbox.
Current CLI versions supporting these options are required; unsupported options
are reported as errors instead of enabling more permissive execution.
.SH SECURITY
The integrated workspace sends captured output to the selected AI provider
through the user's authenticated CLI. Normal account usage applies. AI replies
are displayed as advice and are never executed or typed into the router pane.
Recording\-only install/launch do not invoke an AI provider.
.PP
The log captures everything printed in the terminal. Passwords typed at an
interactive prompt (sudo, SSH password authentication) are
.B not
captured, because the tty does not echo them. Secrets that are visible on
screen
.B are
captured: passwords passed as plaintext arguments, tokens in headers, and the
output of
.B cat
on a .env file or a private key. Use
.B logpause
before such work in a local shell, or F9 in an integrated workspace, including
inside SSH. Use F10 to resume.
.PP
The directory is mode 0700, log files 0600, and a nightly cron job at 03:00
deletes logs older than 30 days.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
terminal-router
terminal-router codex
terminal-router menu
terminal-router launch
terminal-router doctor
.fi
.PP
To reattach to a workspace, replace ID with its directory suffix:
.nf
tmux -S ~/.claude-logs/workspace-ID/tmux.sock attach
.fi
.SH CAVEATS
Removing the package does not undo the per\-user setup, because dpkg cannot
safely edit every user's home directory. Run
.B terminal\-router remove
before removing the package.
.PP
After upgrading, re-run install to update marked blocks with backups.
Existing tmux servers retain loaded hooks; reload ~/.tmux.conf and use
pause/resume in existing panes to replace old pipes.
.PP
Never run
.B terminal\-router
with sudo: as root it would configure
.I /root
instead of your account, and it refuses to do so.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR crontab (5)
.PP
